INSERT INTO public.blog_posts SELECT * FROM jsonb_populate_recordset(null::public.blog_posts, E'[{"id": "7724539d-a123-49d9-97e3-0fe6659348f5", "slug": "how-to-choose-a-physiotherapist-in-rondebosch", "title": "How to Choose a Physiotherapist in Rondebosch: A Practical Guide", "status": "published", "content": "Finding a good physiotherapist in Rondebosch can feel harder than it should, especially when you are sore, busy and not sure what to ask. This guide walks you through what to look for, what happens at a first appointment, and how to make sure you get real value from every session.\\n\\nWhether you have a stiff neck from long hours at a desk, a rolled ankle from a weekend run, or you are recovering from an operation, the right physio can make a big difference. The goal is simple: find someone qualified, easy to get to, and who explains things in a way that makes sense to you.\\n\\n## Why choosing the right physiotherapist in Rondebosch matters\\n\\nPhysiotherapy works best when you and your practitioner work as a team. You will usually see a physio more than once, so trust and good communication matter as much as technical skill. A practice that is close to your home, work or your children''s school also makes it far easier to keep your appointments, and consistency is one of the biggest factors in getting better.\\n\\nRondebosch is well placed for this. It sits close to Newlands, Claremont, Pinelands and the southern suburbs more broadly, so you have options nearby. You can [find a practitioner](/practitioners) on Predictiv and filter by the type of help you need.\\n\\n## What to look for in a physiotherapist\\n\\n### Registration and qualifications\\n\\nEvery physiotherapist practising in South Africa must be registered with the Health Professions Council of South Africa. You can check a practitioner''s registration on the [HPCSA website](https://www.hpcsa.co.za). Many physios are also members of the [South African Society of Physiotherapy](https://saphysio.co.za), which supports ongoing training and professional standards. Membership is not compulsory, but it is a good sign of commitment to the profession.\\n\\n### Experience with your kind of problem\\n\\nPhysiotherapy is a broad field. Some physios focus on sports injuries, some on back and neck pain, others on rehabilitation after surgery, and some on areas like women''s health or children. It is perfectly reasonable to phone a practice and ask whether they often see problems like yours.\\n\\n### Practical things\\n\\n- Location and parking, or how easy it is to get there by taxi or train\\n- Appointment times that suit your schedule, including early mornings or late afternoons\\n- Whether the practice bills your medical aid directly or asks you to pay and claim back\\n- How long a session usually lasts\\n\\n## What happens at your first appointment\\n\\nYour first visit is usually longer than follow-up sessions, because your physio needs to understand the full picture. Expect the appointment to follow roughly this pattern:\\n\\n1. **A conversation.** Your physio will ask when the problem started, what makes it better or worse, how it affects your daily life, and about your general health and medication.\\n2. **A physical assessment.** They will look at how you move, test strength and flexibility, and gently examine the sore area. Wear or bring comfortable clothing that lets them see and reach the area easily.\\n3. **An explanation.** A good physio will tell you, in plain language, what they think is going on and what the plan is. If something is unclear, ask.\\n4. **Treatment.** This may include hands-on techniques, exercises, advice on posture or activity, and sometimes other treatments.\\n5. **A home programme.** Most people leave with a few exercises to do at home. These are often the most important part of your recovery.\\n\\n## Questions to ask your physio\\n\\nDo not be shy to ask questions. A good practitioner will welcome them. Useful ones include:\\n\\n- What do you think is causing my pain?\\n- What can I do at home to help, and what should I avoid?\\n- How many sessions do you expect I will need?\\n- How will we know if treatment is working?\\n- When should I come back, and when should I see a doctor instead?\\n- Will I need to see anyone else later, such as a biokineticist for longer-term strengthening?\\n\\n## How many sessions will you need?\\n\\nThere is no single answer, because it depends on the problem, how long you have had it, your general health and how consistently you do your home exercises. A simple, recent strain may settle within a few sessions. A long-standing problem, or recovery after surgery, often takes longer and may be spread out over several weeks.\\n\\nYour physio should give you a rough idea after the first assessment and review your progress along the way. If you are not seeing any improvement after a few visits, it is fair to ask whether the plan needs to change or whether you should be referred to someone else, such as a GP for further tests.\\n\\n## What to bring to your appointment\\n\\nBeing prepared saves time and helps your physio help you. Bring:\\n\\n- Your ID and medical aid card, if you have one\\n- Any referral letter from a doctor, although you do not usually need one to see a physio\\n- Recent X-rays, scans or reports related to the problem\\n- A list of your current medication\\n- Comfortable clothing, such as shorts for a knee or hip problem, or a vest top for a shoulder problem\\n- Notes about when the pain started and what makes it worse\\n\\nMedical aid cover for physiotherapy depends on your plan, so check your benefits before you go. Some plans pay from savings, others from a specific benefit, and some need pre-authorisation for certain treatments.\\n\\n## When to see a doctor or seek urgent care first\\n\\nA physiotherapist in Rondebosch can help with most everyday muscle and joint problems, but some symptoms need medical attention first. See a GP or go to an emergency unit if you have:\\n\\n- Pain after a major fall, car accident or other serious injury\\n- Loss of bladder or bowel control, or numbness in the saddle area (between your legs)\\n- Fever, feeling very unwell, or unexplained weight loss alongside your pain\\n- Chest pain, sudden severe headache or sudden weakness in an arm or leg\\n- A joint that is hot, very swollen, or that you cannot put weight on\\n\\nFor any emergency, call an ambulance or go straight to your nearest emergency unit. If you are not sure whether you need a doctor first, you can [see GPs in Rondebosch](/practitioners/gps/rondebosch) on Predictiv.\\n\\n## Find a physiotherapist in Rondebosch with Predictiv\\n\\nPredictiv is a free Cape Town platform that helps you find practitioners nearby and understand which type of practitioner might suit a simple problem. It offers directional guidance only and never a diagnosis. Browse [physiotherapists in Rondebosch](/practitioners/physiotherapists/rondebosch), or if you are not sure who to see, [describe your problem](/assistant) and we will point you in a sensible direction.", "excerpt": "What to look for in a physiotherapist in Rondebosch, what happens at your first appointment, and how to get the most from your sessions.", "created_at": "2026-09-17T06:47:37.95928+00:00", "meta_title": "How to Choose a Physiotherapist in Rondebosch", "updated_at": "2026-09-17T06:47:37.95928+00:00", "author_name": "Predictiv", "published_at": "2026-09-16T20:47:37.95928+00:00", "target_keyword": "physiotherapist in Rondebosch", "cover_image_url": null, "meta_description": "Looking for a physiotherapist in Rondebosch? Learn what to check, what happens at your first visit, questions to ask and what to bring."}, {"id": "590bb3a9-7c10-4a6d-b13e-2e3d15681b24", "slug": "physio-or-chiro-for-back-pain", "title": "Physio or Chiro for Back Pain? How to Choose the Right Help", "status": "published", "content": "If you have a sore back, you may be wondering whether to book a physio or chiro for back pain. Both can help with many common back problems, and the best choice often depends on the type of pain, what you prefer and what is available near you.\\n\\nBack pain is one of the most common reasons adults seek help, and most everyday back pain settles well with the right advice, movement and time. This guide explains how physiotherapists and chiropractors differ, where they overlap, and when you should see a GP or a biokineticist instead.\\n\\n## What a physiotherapist does for back pain\\n\\nPhysiotherapists are movement and rehabilitation specialists. In South Africa they are registered with the [Health Professions Council of South Africa](https://www.hpcsa.co.za), and many belong to the [South African Society of Physiotherapy](https://saphysio.co.za).\\n\\nFor back pain, a physio will usually:\\n\\n- Assess how you move, sit, bend and lift\\n- Use hands-on techniques such as massage, mobilisation and sometimes manipulation\\n- Teach you specific exercises to reduce pain and restore movement\\n- Give advice on posture, work setup, sleep and staying active\\n- Build a plan that gradually gets you back to normal activity\\n\\nPhysiotherapy tends to have a strong focus on exercise and helping you manage the problem yourself over time. Physios also treat a very wide range of conditions beyond the spine, including sports injuries and recovery after surgery.\\n\\n## What a chiropractor does for back pain\\n\\nChiropractors focus on the spine, joints and nervous system. In South Africa they are registered with the [Allied Health Professions Council of South Africa](https://www.ahpcsa.co.za), and many are members of the [Chiropractic Association of South Africa](https://chiropractic.co.za). South African chiropractors complete a lengthy university qualification that includes training in diagnosis and when to refer.\\n\\nFor back pain, a chiropractor will usually:\\n\\n- Take a history and examine your spine, posture and nerves\\n- Use spinal manipulation or mobilisation to improve joint movement\\n- Use soft tissue techniques to ease muscle tension\\n- Often give exercises and lifestyle advice as well\\n\\nChiropractic care is often best known for spinal manipulation, the quick, controlled movement that sometimes produces a clicking sound. Many people find it gives welcome relief, particularly for recent, uncomplicated back or neck pain.\\n\\n## Where physios and chiros overlap\\n\\nIn practice, there is a lot of common ground. Both professions:\\n\\n- Are university trained and registered with a statutory council\\n- Can assess back pain and recognise signs that need a doctor\\n- Use hands-on treatment and exercise\\n- Aim to reduce pain and get you moving again\\n\\nThe style of treatment can vary more between individual practitioners than between the two professions. Some physios use a lot of manipulation, and some chiropractors put a big emphasis on exercise. That is why it helps to ask a practice how they usually approach back pain.\\n\\n## Physio or chiro for back pain: how to decide\\n\\nThere is no single right answer, but these pointers can help you choose between a physio or chiro for back pain:\\n\\n1. **Consider the type of problem.** For a recent, stiff, \\"locked\\" back or neck without other symptoms, either can be a good choice. For back pain linked to a sports injury, recovery after surgery or a problem affecting several parts of the body, a physio is often a natural fit.\\n2. **Think about what you prefer.** If you want a strong focus on exercise and self-management, many physios work this way. If you have had good results with spinal manipulation before, a chiropractor may suit you.\\n3. **Check what is practical.** Location, appointment times and medical aid cover all matter. Cover for both professions depends on your plan, so check your benefits.\\n4. **Look for good communication.** Whoever you choose should explain what they think is happening, what the plan is and roughly how long it should take.\\n5. **Review your progress.** If you are not improving after a few sessions, ask whether the plan should change or whether you need a referral.\\n\\nStill unsure? You can [describe your problem](/assistant) on Predictiv for directional guidance on which type of practitioner may suit you. It is not a diagnosis, but it can help you take a sensible first step.\\n\\nRemember that the choice between a physio or chiro for back pain is rarely permanent. If one approach is not helping, it is reasonable to try the other or ask for a referral.\\n\\n## When to see a GP first\\n\\nSometimes a doctor is the better starting point. Book a GP if:\\n\\n- You feel generally unwell along with your back pain\\n- The pain started without any obvious reason and is getting steadily worse\\n- You have a history of cancer, osteoporosis or a weakened immune system\\n- The pain is severe at night and does not ease with rest or changing position\\n- You need medication, a sick note, blood tests or scans\\n- Pain is spreading down a leg with numbness, pins and needles or weakness\\n\\nA GP can examine you, arrange tests if needed and refer you on. You can find [GPs near you](/practitioners/gps) on Predictiv.\\n\\n## Red flags: when to seek urgent care\\n\\nSome symptoms can point to a serious problem. Go to an emergency unit or call an ambulance straight away if your back pain comes with:\\n\\n- Loss of bladder or bowel control, or difficulty passing urine\\n- Numbness in the saddle area (between your legs, around your genitals or buttocks)\\n- Weakness in both legs, or weakness that is getting worse quickly\\n- Fever, chills or feeling very unwell\\n- Unexplained weight loss\\n- Pain after a major fall, car accident or other significant injury\\n\\nDo not wait to book a regular appointment if you have any of these symptoms.\\n\\n## When a biokineticist can help later\\n\\nOnce the worst of the pain has settled, a biokineticist can be very helpful. Biokineticists are HPCSA registered exercise specialists who focus on the final phase of rehabilitation and long-term conditioning. They design structured exercise programmes to build strength, endurance and control, which can help you return to sport, work and daily life with more confidence.\\n\\nMany people move from a physio or chiropractor to a biokineticist once they no longer need hands-on treatment. You can learn more through the [Biokinetics Association of South Africa](https://biokineticssa.org.za) or browse [biokineticists](/practitioners/biokineticists) on Predictiv.\\n\\n## Find the right practitioner near you\\n\\nPredictiv is a free Cape Town platform that helps you find physiotherapists, chiropractors, biokineticists and GPs near you, starting in the southern suburbs. Browse [physiotherapists](/practitioners/physiotherapists) or [chiropractors](/practitioners/chiropractors), or [find a practitioner](/practitioners) in your area and take the first step towards a stronger, more comfortable back.", "excerpt": "Physiotherapists and chiropractors can both help a sore back. Here is how they differ, how to choose, and when a GP or biokineticist is the better fit.", "created_at": "2026-09-17T06:48:25.210846+00:00", "meta_title": "Physio or Chiro for Back Pain? How to Decide", "updated_at": "2026-09-17T06:48:25.210846+00:00", "author_name": "Predictiv", "published_at": "2026-09-16T22:48:25.210846+00:00", "target_keyword": "physio or chiro for back pain", "cover_image_url": null, "meta_description": "Not sure whether to see a physio or chiro for back pain? Learn the differences, the overlaps, red flags and when to see a GP first."}, {"id": "dc9d076f-fea6-4c61-87c7-0c705155d51f", "slug": "what-does-a-biokineticist-do", "title": "What Does a Biokineticist Do? A Plain-Language Guide", "status": "published", "content": "If you have been referred to one and wondered what does a biokineticist do, you are not alone. Biokinetics is a uniquely South African profession, so many people have never heard of it until a doctor, physio or friend recommends it.\\n\\nIn simple terms, a biokineticist is an exercise specialist who uses carefully planned, scientifically based exercise to improve health, recover from injury and manage long-term conditions. This guide explains how it works, who it helps and what a session looks like.\\n\\n## What does a biokineticist do?\\n\\nA biokineticist assesses how your body moves and functions, then designs an exercise programme to meet your specific goals. The focus is on physical activity as treatment. Rather than doing things to you, a biokineticist teaches and guides you to do the work yourself, safely and progressively.\\n\\nBiokineticists typically help with:\\n\\n- The final phase of rehabilitation after an injury or surgery\\n- Improving strength, balance, flexibility and endurance\\n- Managing chronic conditions through exercise\\n- Improving posture and movement patterns\\n- Helping people return to sport or work with confidence\\n- Preventing future injuries\\n\\n## A uniquely South African profession\\n\\nBiokinetics developed in South Africa, and the title is specific to this country. Biokineticists complete a university degree in human movement or sport science, followed by an honours degree in biokinetics and a supervised internship. They must be registered with the [Health Professions Council of South Africa](https://www.hpcsa.co.za) to practise, just like doctors and physiotherapists.\\n\\nMany biokineticists are also members of the [Biokinetics Association of South Africa](https://biokineticssa.org.za), which supports professional standards and ongoing learning. When you choose a biokineticist, it is worth confirming their HPCSA registration.\\n\\nIf you are still asking what does a biokineticist do in practice, think of them as the bridge between treatment and everyday life. They help you move from simply being out of pain to being strong, fit and confident in your body again. Their work is built on exercise science, so every programme is planned, measured and adjusted over time rather than guessed.\\n\\n## Conditions a biokineticist can help with\\n\\nBecause exercise benefits so many parts of the body, biokineticists work with a wide range of people. Common areas include:\\n\\n### Orthopaedic and sports problems\\n\\n- Recovery after knee, shoulder, hip or ankle injuries\\n- Rehabilitation after operations such as ligament reconstruction or joint replacement\\n- Ongoing back and neck pain, once acute pain has settled\\n- Recurring sports injuries\\n\\n### Chronic health conditions\\n\\n- High blood pressure\\n- Type 2 diabetes\\n- Raised cholesterol\\n- Weight management\\n- Osteoporosis and low bone density\\n- Some heart and lung conditions, usually with medical clearance\\n\\n### Other groups\\n\\n- Older adults wanting to improve balance and reduce the risk of falls\\n- Pregnant and postnatal women, where appropriate\\n- Children and teenagers with posture or coordination concerns\\n- People who want to start exercising safely after a long break\\n\\nIf you have a medical condition, your biokineticist may ask for information from your doctor before starting, to make sure your programme is safe.\\n\\n## How a biokineticist differs from a physiotherapist\\n\\nPeople often confuse the two, and there is some overlap. Both are HPCSA registered and both use exercise. The main differences are in emphasis and timing:\\n\\n- **Physiotherapists** often see you earlier, when pain and injury are more acute. They commonly use hands-on treatment, pain relief techniques and early rehabilitation exercises.\\n- **Biokineticists** usually take over once the acute phase has passed. They focus on building strength, fitness and function through progressive exercise, with little or no hands-on treatment.\\n\\nA common path is physio first, then biokineticist. For example, after a knee operation, a physio may help you regain basic movement and walking, and a biokineticist then helps you rebuild full strength and return to running or sport. The two professions often work closely together. You can learn more about physiotherapy through the [South African Society of Physiotherapy](https://saphysio.co.za).\\n\\n## What a session looks like\\n\\nYour first appointment is usually an assessment. It may include:\\n\\n1. **A health history.** Questions about your injury or condition, medication, activity levels and goals.\\n2. **Measurements.** These may include blood pressure, heart rate, body composition and posture.\\n3. **Movement and fitness tests.** Strength, flexibility, balance and endurance, adapted to your level.\\n4. **Goal setting.** Agreeing on realistic short and longer term goals.\\n\\nFrom there, your biokineticist designs a programme. Follow-up sessions usually take place in a well-equipped exercise space, often one on one or in small groups. You will work through exercises under supervision, and your programme is adjusted as you improve. Most people also receive exercises to do at home or in a gym.\\n\\nWear comfortable clothes and proper training shoes, and bring water and a towel. Bring any reports, scans or referral letters you have.\\n\\n## How long does treatment take?\\n\\nThis depends on your goals, your condition and how consistent you are. Exercise-based improvement takes time, so biokinetics is usually a programme over a number of weeks rather than a single visit. Your biokineticist should reassess you along the way so you can see your progress.\\n\\nMedical aid cover for biokinetics depends on your plan. Some plans cover it from savings or a specific benefit, and some require a referral. Check your benefits before you start.\\n\\n## When to see someone else first\\n\\nA biokineticist is not the right first stop for every problem. See a GP or physiotherapist first if you have a new, very painful injury. Seek urgent medical care, by calling an ambulance or going to an emergency unit, if you have chest pain, severe shortness of breath, pain after a major fall, or back pain with loss of bladder or bowel control or numbness in the saddle area. You can find [GPs near you](/practitioners/gps) on Predictiv if you need a doctor first.\\n\\n## Find a biokineticist near you with Predictiv\\n\\nNow that you know what does a biokineticist do, the next step is finding one close to home. Predictiv is a free Cape Town platform that helps you find practitioners nearby. Browse [biokineticists](/practitioners/biokineticists), including [biokineticists in Newlands](/practitioners/biokineticists/newlands) and [biokineticists in Claremont](/practitioners/biokineticists/claremont), or [describe your problem](/assistant) for directional guidance on who to see.", "excerpt": "Biokinetics is a uniquely South African profession. Here is what biokineticists do, who they help and how they differ from physiotherapists.", "created_at": "2026-09-17T06:49:21.143826+00:00", "meta_title": "What Does a Biokineticist Do? A Simple Guide", "updated_at": "2026-09-17T06:49:21.143826+00:00", "author_name": "Predictiv", "published_at": "2026-09-17T00:49:21.143826+00:00", "target_keyword": "what does a biokineticist do", "cover_image_url": null, "meta_description": "What does a biokineticist do? Learn how this South African exercise profession helps with injuries and chronic conditions, and what to expect."}, {"id": "22154732-8c72-4534-9af9-af9a1331186f", "slug": "knee-pain-in-cape-town-who-to-see", "title": "Knee Pain in Cape Town: Who to See and When", "status": "published", "content": "Knee pain in Cape Town is something many active people deal with at some point, whether it comes from a trail run, a hike up Table Mountain, a game of soccer or simply getting older. The good news is that most knee problems respond well to the right care, and knowing who to see first can save you time, money and frustration.\\n\\nThis guide explains which practitioner usually suits different types of knee pain, the warning signs that need urgent attention, and how recovery often moves from physiotherapy to biokinetics.\\n\\n## Why knee pain in Cape Town is so common\\n\\nCape Town is a city that loves to move. People run along the promenade and through the southern suburbs, hike the mountain and its many trails, cycle, surf and play club sport. All of that activity is great for health, but it also puts the knees to work. Steep descents on hiking trails, sudden increases in running distance and twisting movements in sport are all common triggers.\\n\\nKnee pain also has many causes that have nothing to do with sport, such as age-related changes in the joint, weight, previous injuries and the way we sit and move during the day. Whatever the cause, the first step is working out what kind of knee pain you have.\\n\\n## Who to see for different types of knee pain\\n\\nThe advice below is directional only. It is not a diagnosis, and a registered practitioner should assess you properly.\\n\\n### Sudden injury\\n\\nIf you twisted your knee, heard a pop, or it swelled up soon after an injury, you may have damaged a ligament, cartilage or other structure.\\n\\n- If you cannot put weight on the leg, the knee is badly swollen or it looks deformed, go to an emergency unit.\\n- If you can walk, but the knee is sore and swollen, a GP or a physiotherapist is a sensible first stop. They can assess the knee and refer you for scans or to a specialist if needed.\\n- In the first day or two, relative rest, ice, gentle compression and keeping the leg raised can help ease swelling.\\n\\n### Running and overuse pain\\n\\nPain that builds up gradually, often around or behind the kneecap or on the outside of the knee, is common in runners, hikers and cyclists. It is often linked to training load, muscle strength and movement patterns.\\n\\n- A physiotherapist is usually a good first choice. They can assess how you move, treat the sore area and adjust your training.\\n- A biokineticist can then help build strength and control in the hips, thighs and calves to reduce the chance of the problem coming back.\\n- Look at recent changes, such as more mileage, more hills, new shoes or a new sport.\\n\\n### Age-related stiffness and aching\\n\\nStiffness in the morning, aching after activity and a sense that the knee is \\"creaky\\" are common as we get older, and are often related to wear in the joint.\\n\\n- A GP can help confirm what is going on and discuss pain management.\\n- A physiotherapist can help with pain relief, movement and a suitable exercise plan.\\n- A biokineticist is very helpful for long-term strengthening, balance and staying active, which is one of the most effective ways to manage stiff knees.\\n\\n### Recovery after surgery\\n\\nAfter a knee operation, such as a ligament repair, meniscus surgery or joint replacement, your surgeon will usually guide your rehabilitation.\\n\\n- Physiotherapy often starts early, to reduce swelling, restore movement and get you walking well.\\n- A biokineticist commonly takes over in the later stages, to rebuild full strength, confidence and fitness for sport or daily life.\\n- Always follow your surgeon''s specific instructions and time frames.\\n\\n## Red flags for knee pain\\n\\nSome knee symptoms need urgent medical attention. Call an ambulance or go to an emergency unit if:\\n\\n- You cannot bear weight on the leg after an injury\\n- The knee is hot, red and very swollen, especially if you also have a fever or feel unwell\\n- The knee is locked and you cannot straighten it\\n- The knee looks deformed or the kneecap has moved out of place\\n- Your calf is painful, swollen and warm, particularly after surgery, a long flight or a long period of sitting\\n- You have numbness, coldness or a change in colour in the lower leg or foot\\n\\nFor less urgent but worrying symptoms, such as swelling that keeps coming back or a knee that often gives way, book a GP or physiotherapist soon.\\n\\n## The path from physio to biokineticist\\n\\nFor many people with knee pain in Cape Town, recovery follows a similar path:\\n\\n1. **Assessment.** A GP or physiotherapist works out what is likely going on and whether you need scans or a specialist.\\n2. **Early treatment.** A physiotherapist helps settle pain and swelling, restores movement and starts basic strengthening.\\n3. **Rebuilding.** A biokineticist designs a progressive exercise programme to build strength, balance and endurance.\\n4. **Return to activity.** You gradually return to running, hiking or sport, with a plan to reduce the risk of future injury.\\n\\nPhysiotherapists and biokineticists in South Africa are both registered with the [Health Professions Council of South Africa](https://www.hpcsa.co.za). You can read more about each profession through the [South African Society of Physiotherapy](https://saphysio.co.za) and the [Biokinetics Association of South Africa](https://biokineticssa.org.za).\\n\\n## Tips to protect your knees\\n\\n- Increase running or hiking distance gradually rather than all at once\\n- Strengthen your hips, thighs and calves, not just your knees\\n- Take care on steep descents, and consider hiking poles on long mountain routes\\n- Replace worn-out running shoes\\n- Warm up before sport and cool down afterwards\\n- Listen to early warning signs rather than pushing through sharp pain\\n\\nMedical aid cover for physiotherapy, biokinetics and GP visits depends on your plan, so check your benefits.\\n\\n## Get help for knee pain in Cape Town with Predictiv\\n\\nPredictiv is a free platform that helps you find practitioners near you, starting in Rondebosch, Claremont, Newlands, Pinelands, Kenilworth, Wynberg and Plumstead. If you are dealing with knee pain in Cape Town, browse [physiotherapists in Newlands](/practitioners/physiotherapists/newlands), [biokineticists in Kenilworth](/practitioners/biokineticists/kenilworth) or [GPs](/practitioners/gps). Not sure where to start? [Describe your problem](/assistant) and get directional guidance on the right type of practitioner.", "excerpt": "From trail running injuries to age-related stiffness, here is who to see for knee pain in Cape Town and the warning signs that need urgent care.", "created_at": "2026-09-17T06:49:45.958552+00:00", "meta_title": "Knee Pain in Cape Town: Who Should You See?", "updated_at": "2026-09-17T06:49:45.958552+00:00", "author_name": "Predictiv", "published_at": "2026-09-17T02:49:45.958552+00:00", "target_keyword": "knee pain in Cape Town", "cover_image_url": null, "meta_description": "Dealing with knee pain in Cape Town? Find out who to see for sudden injuries, running pain, stiffness or recovery after surgery, plus red flags."}, {"id": "4f5d913b-15a0-4647-981e-38ff6ed5fe9e", "slug": "seeing-a-chiropractor-in-claremont-what-to-expect", "title": "Seeing a Chiropractor in Claremont: What to Expect", "status": "published", "content": "If you are thinking about seeing a chiropractor in Claremont, it helps to know what to expect before you book. Understanding what chiropractors treat, how they are trained and what happens at a first visit will help you feel more comfortable and make a confident choice.\\n\\nClaremont is a busy hub in Cape Town''s southern suburbs, with easy access from Rondebosch, Kenilworth, Newlands and Wynberg. That makes it a convenient place to find care, whether you work nearby or live close by.\\n\\n## What chiropractors do\\n\\nChiropractors are healthcare practitioners who focus on problems with the spine, joints, muscles and nervous system. They assess how your body moves and use hands-on techniques to improve movement and reduce pain.\\n\\nCommon treatment methods include:\\n\\n- **Spinal manipulation.** A quick, controlled movement to a joint, sometimes with a clicking sound\\n- **Mobilisation.** Gentler, slower movements to ease stiff joints\\n- **Soft tissue work.** Techniques to relax tight muscles\\n- **Exercise and advice.** Stretches, strengthening exercises and guidance on posture, work setup and daily habits\\n\\nNot every chiropractor uses every technique, and a good practitioner will adapt treatment to your needs and preferences.\\n\\n## What a chiropractor in Claremont can help with\\n\\nChiropractors commonly see people with:\\n\\n- Lower back pain\\n- Neck pain and stiffness\\n- Some types of headaches, particularly those linked to the neck\\n- Upper back and shoulder tension\\n- Joint pain in areas such as the hips or shoulders\\n- Aches linked to posture, desk work or driving\\n\\nIf your problem is not something a chiropractor can help with, they should tell you and refer you to someone more suitable, such as a GP or physiotherapist.\\n\\n## Training, registration and safety\\n\\nIn South Africa, chiropractors complete a university programme that includes training in anatomy, physiology, diagnosis and when to refer patients on. To practise, they must be registered with the [Allied Health Professions Council of South Africa](https://www.ahpcsa.co.za). You can check a practitioner''s registration with the council before your first visit.\\n\\nMany chiropractors are also members of the [Chiropractic Association of South Africa](https://chiropractic.co.za), which promotes professional standards and ongoing education.\\n\\n### Is chiropractic treatment safe?\\n\\nFor most people, treatment from a registered chiropractor is considered safe. Some people feel mild soreness or stiffness for a day or two after treatment, similar to how you may feel after exercise. Serious complications are rare.\\n\\nTo keep treatment as safe as possible:\\n\\n- Tell your chiropractor about all your health conditions and medication\\n- Mention if you have osteoporosis, a history of cancer, a blood clotting problem or have had recent surgery\\n- Let them know if you feel dizzy, numb or unwell during or after treatment\\n- Remember that you can always ask for a gentler technique or decline a specific treatment\\n\\n## What to expect at your first visit\\n\\nYour first appointment is usually longer than follow-up visits. It often includes:\\n\\n1. **A detailed history.** Your chiropractor will ask about your symptoms, when they started, what makes them better or worse, your general health and your lifestyle.\\n2. **An examination.** This usually includes checking your posture, movement, reflexes, muscle strength and sensation, and gently feeling the painful area.\\n3. **Further tests if needed.** In some cases, your chiropractor may recommend X-rays or refer you to a doctor for other tests.\\n4. **An explanation and plan.** They should explain what they think is causing your pain, what treatment they suggest, how many visits they expect and what you can do at home.\\n5. **Treatment.** Many people receive their first treatment on the same day, once you have agreed to the plan.\\n\\nWear comfortable clothing and bring any relevant scans, reports or referral letters, as well as your medical aid card. Medical aid cover for chiropractic care depends on your plan, so check your benefits before your visit.\\n\\n## How to choose a chiropractor in Claremont\\n\\nA few practical points can help you find the right fit:\\n\\n- **Check registration.** Make sure they are registered with the AHPCSA.\\n- **Ask about their approach.** Some chiropractors focus mainly on manipulation, while others include more exercise and rehabilitation.\\n- **Look for clear communication.** You should understand your treatment plan and feel free to ask questions.\\n- **Be cautious of long upfront commitments.** A reasonable plan is reviewed regularly based on your progress.\\n- **Consider convenience.** Location, parking and appointment times all make it easier to stay on track.\\n\\nIt is also fair to compare. Chiropractors, physiotherapists and biokineticists can all help with back and neck problems in different ways. You can browse [physiotherapists](/practitioners/physiotherapists) and [biokineticists in Claremont](/practitioners/biokineticists/claremont) on Predictiv to see your options.\\n\\n### Questions to ask before you start\\n\\n- What do you think is causing my pain?\\n- How many visits do you expect, and when will we review progress?\\n- What can I do at home to help?\\n- What should I do if I feel worse after treatment?\\n- When would you refer me to a GP or another practitioner?\\n\\n## When to see a doctor or seek urgent care\\n\\nA chiropractor is not the right first stop for every problem. See a GP first if you feel generally unwell, have unexplained weight loss, or your pain is getting steadily worse without a clear cause. You can find [GPs in Claremont](/practitioners/gps/claremont) on Predictiv.\\n\\nCall an ambulance or go to an emergency unit immediately if you have:\\n\\n- Loss of bladder or bowel control\\n- Numbness in the saddle area (between your legs)\\n- Weakness in the legs that is getting worse\\n- Fever along with back or neck pain\\n- Pain after a major fall or accident\\n- A sudden, severe headache, or neck pain with dizziness, slurred speech or trouble seeing\\n\\n## Find a chiropractor in Claremont with Predictiv\\n\\nPredictiv is a free Cape Town platform that helps you find practitioners near you and understand which type of practitioner may suit a simple problem. It gives directional guidance only, never a diagnosis. Browse [chiropractors in Claremont](/practitioners/chiropractors/claremont) to find a chiropractor in Claremont, or [describe your problem](/assistant) if you are not sure who to see first.", "excerpt": "What a chiropractor in Claremont can help with, how they are registered, and what happens at your first visit.", "created_at": "2026-09-17T06:50:14.56841+00:00", "meta_title": "Chiropractor in Claremont: What to Expect", "updated_at": "2026-09-17T06:50:14.56841+00:00", "author_name": "Predictiv", "published_at": "2026-09-17T04:50:14.56841+00:00", "target_keyword": "chiropractor in Claremont", "cover_image_url": null, "meta_description": "Thinking of seeing a chiropractor in Claremont? Learn what chiropractors treat, how safe it is, what happens at a first visit and how to choose."}]'::jsonb) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;
